1
00:00:00,826 --> 00:00:03,769
An overwrite can fail when a write limit is reached.

2
00:00:03,769 --> 00:00:06,486
A safe upload should leave the old file intact.

3
00:00:06,486 --> 00:00:08,297
Our tiny original is ten bytes.

4
00:00:08,297 --> 00:00:11,241
The attempted replacement is nine hundred thousand.

5
00:01:14,373 --> 00:01:16,581
The server reports file too large.

6
00:01:16,581 --> 00:01:20,347
The original is replaced by half a million partial bytes.

7
00:01:20,347 --> 00:01:21,322
Upload failed.

8
00:01:21,322 --> 00:01:24,568
Unfortunately, the old file failed to remain old.

9
00:01:26,395 --> 00:01:29,722
Two successful upload controls establish the baseline.

10
00:01:29,722 --> 00:01:33,171
A controlled write-size limit then induces the failure.

11
00:01:33,171 --> 00:01:35,020
The original content is lost.

12
00:01:35,020 --> 00:01:37,915
This high severity defect was confirmed twice.
